// A `_` leaf of a destructuring `let` consumes a reference ONLY when the source
// handed one over.
//
// The reviewer's repro for the SL-275 follow-up. `visit_DestructuringLet` was
// fixed to pass the shared transfer oracle's source-ownership answer, and
// `_destructure_walk` honoured it for `BindingPattern` while the
// `WildcardPattern` arm queued the RAW projected component for an
// unconditional drop. Over a borrowed projection that component is still owned
// by its container, so the discard released a reference the destructure never
// acquired — the named leaf's retain and the wildcard's spurious release
// cancelled out, which is why the count below looked plausible.
//
// COUNTS, derived rather than observed: `base` is one reference, and `Pair`
// holds two more, so `before` is 3. `let (kept, _) = p.t` reads a projection
// the container keeps owning, so `kept` takes its OWN reference (4) and the `_`
// leaf takes none and releases none. All three readings are the same `Arc`, so
// `after` is `4 4 4`. Pre-fix it printed `after 3 3 3`.
//
// The single-shot shape understates it: each repetition leaked one release, so
// a loop over the same borrowed projection walked the count DOWN (3, 2, 1) and
// then read 7 out of freed storage with the container still live. The `repeat`
// row is that shape — three calls that each destructure the same projection and
// let their bindings die at the call's scope exit. It reads 5 inside every call
// (`base` 1 + `Pair` 2 + main's `kept` 1 + the call's own `kept` 1) and the
// value must be the SAME all three times; pre-fix it walked 4, 3, 2.
//
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: before 3
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: after 4 4 4
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: repeat 5 5 5
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: end 4

struct Res { id: Int }
struct Pair { t: (Arc<Res>, Arc<Res>) }
@synthesize
extension Pair: Copy {}

// Each call destructures the SAME borrowed projection and lets its bindings die
// at the call's scope exit, so a leaked release shows up as a count that walks.
func round(p: &Pair) -> Int {
    let (kept, _) = p.t
    kept.strong_count()
}

func main() {
    let base = try! Arc(value: Res(id: 1))
    let p = Pair(t: (base.copy(), base.copy()))
    print("before {}", base.strong_count())
    let (kept, _) = p.t
    print("after {} {} {}", base.strong_count(), kept.strong_count(),
          p.t.1.strong_count())

    let r1 = round(&p)
    let r2 = round(&p)
    let r3 = round(&p)
    print("repeat {} {} {}", r1, r2, r3)
    print("end {}", p.t.0.strong_count())
}
